## ABSTRACT
In this study, an alkynyl-modified aromatic dicarboxylic acid bifunctional ligand was selected to construct lanthanide compound \(\{[\mathsf{E u}_{4}(\mathsf{e b d c})_{6}(4,4\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b p y})_{0.5}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{4.5}]\cdot(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{5}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H})_{1.25}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})\}_{n}\) (Eu-MOF, \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{e b d c}=5\) -ethynyl-isophthalic acid, 4,4-bpy = 4,4-bipyridine, and MOF = metal-organic framework), of which the uncoordinated alkynyl group would be used to anchor silver nanoclusters (Ag NCs). The Eu-MOF exhibits double emission peaks, located at 492 and \(611\,\textrm{n m}\), respectively, in which the high-energy blue emission is associated with alkynyl-modified ligand while the low-energy red emission belongs to characteristic emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\), indicating that ligands can effectively sensitize \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) luminescence. The intensity ratio of the dual emission fluorescence peaks of Eu-MOF displays a good linear relationship with temperature, which realizes the detection function in the low temperature region of \(75{-}275\ \mathsf{K}\), and the thermal sensitivity reaches \(1.5398\%\cdot\mathsf{K}^{-1}\). After anchoring the Ag NCs, the high-energy blue emission is significantly quenched, indicating that the Ag NCs are indeed confined into the framework and interact with the alkynyl group, and thus change the overall electronic distribution. This is the first case of anchoring Ag NCs by a luminescent Eu-MOF and studying nanocluster loading by using spectroscopic properties. In addition, the Ag NCs@Eu-MOF also shows a good catalytic activity for cycloaddition reaction from \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and epoxides. This study not only provides ideas for exploring the changes in optical properties of luminescent MOFs and Ag NCs caused by confinement effect, but also expands their potential applications in various fields.
## KEYWORDS
silver nanoclusters, confinement effect, lanthanide metal-organic framework (MOF), ratiometric fluorescence thermometer, \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) cycloaddition
## 1 Introduction
Noble metal nanoclusters [1, 2], characterized by ultra-small size, discrete electron energy levels, and unique molecular-like features [3], have been a hot research spot that draws researchers' attention because of their particular luminescent [4, 5], catalytic [6, 7], chiral [8], magnetic [9], and electrochemical properties [10]. Among the large number of luminescent noble metal nanoclusters, silver nanoclusters (Ag NCs) are well known for their many advantages such as unique electronic states and optical properties [11–15], which have been potentially useful candidates for applications in various fields such as fluorescence sensor and catalysis. Hence, the synthesis of silver nanocluster with delocalized electrons has been the focus of research interest [16–18]. However, contrary to common nanomaterials like mesoporous silica [19] and graphene [20], the Ag NCs with delocalized electrons are confronted with that situation of easy oxidation or instability, which greatly impedes their further application. They are sensitive to environment, easy to decompose or aggregate affected by some external factors such as light conditions, temperatures, and pH, and satisfactory applications can not be obtained in luminescence and catalysis [21–23]. Therefore, the rational synthesis of stable Ag NCs remains a challenge.
