Generally, to deal with this dilemma, small Ag NCs can be confined in zeolites [24, 25], metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [26–28], or other systems [29, 30] based on the confinement effect, which improves their stability to a certain extent. MOF, with the comprehensive superiority of strong designability, high flexibility, and easy modification of structures, has been extensively used as a host material for confining noble metal nanoparticles (NPs) or nanoclusters [31–38]. In 2014, based on post-confinement strategy, for the first time, Jiang's group first introduced the metal precursors of \(\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) into the mesoporous \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\) Materials Institute Lavoisier, MIL-101 by the double solvents approach, followed by in-situ reduction, and successfully realized the encapsulation of bimetallic core-shell nanoparticles by mesoporous MOF under mild conditions [39]. Benefited from the confinement effect of MIL-101, the resultant \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Co}\) NPs were superior to their monometallic counterparts and alloy NPs in terms of better economy and easy recycle and effectively avoid the problem of easy aggregation of small NPs into large size NPs. At the same time, the composite \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{MIL}-101\) exhibited
excellent catalytic activity and cycling stability in hydrolytic dehydrogenation of ammonia borane reaction. In 2021, Fei and coworkers, adopting the method of post-confinement synthesis, successfully obtained ultrasmall and highly dispersed gold nanoclusters stabilized with N-heterocyclic carbenes (NHCs) ligand in parent University of Oslo, UiO-68-NHC [40]. And the resultant Au-NC@UiO-68-NHC composite not only exhibited high photostability and excellent chemical stability, but also showed a favourable and stable activity in photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, obviously surpassing the control group without NHC-ligand stabilization. The confined growth of metal nanoclusters in MOFs provides a viable approach for the development of stable clusters and also offers greater possibilities for their potential application. At present, MOFs have been used as the suitable matrix for confining noble metal nanoclusters, where Au clusters are in the majority [33,34,40], and are mainly focused on the catalysis study. Comparatively speaking, it was rarely reported about metal clusters confined by lanthanide MOFs, especially Ag NCs. The challenge of effective confinement methods and how to take advantage of MOFs to confine nanoclusters still exist.
In the synthesis and study of noble metal nanoclusters, the alkynyl ligand is a relatively new ligand, containing \(\pi\) bonds and \(\sigma\) bonds to form a conjugate system of the whole metal cluster, which can combine organic carbon and inorganic metal well, so as to obtain a stable configuration [41, 42]. Generally, the reactivity of alkynyl and its derivatives is relatively low because of sphybridization carbon atoms, so the activation of \(C{\equiv}C\) bond is a critical step in their synthesis and transformation. Due to the excellent \(\pi\) -Lewis acidity, and excellent affinity for carbon and proton-like properties, silver(I) is peculiarly prone to replace the acidic hydrogen of the terminal alkyne, thus activating the \(C{\equiv}C\) bond via \(\sigma\) -coordination to form silver acetylide [43]. The relatively inexpensive silver reagents are an idealistic option for the activation of \(C{\equiv}C\) bond in comparison with other noble metals. Furthermore, the Ag NCs stabilized by alkynyl ligands possess peculiar electronic structure and optical properties [44]. To the best of our knowledge, there are no reports of confinement of silver clusters by using alkynyl ligands-containing lanthanide MOFs, which opens a door for studying the change of luminescence behaviour triggered by the mutual interactions between guest Ag NCs and host luminescent MOFs.
In this work, we adopted bifunctional ligands, 5-ethynylisophthalic acid (denoted as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ebdc}\)) with dicarboxylic and alkynyl groups to construct lanthanide luminescent MOF materials (denoted as Eu-MOF) by solvothermal method, and the resultant composite Ag NCs@Eu-MOF through a simple post-synthetic modification strategy (Scheme 1). The Eu-MOF shows dual emission properties of both ligands and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions, and good temperature-dependent photoluminescence, which is a good candidate for a ratiometric fluorescent thermometer. The changes of optical properties and thermal stability of host Eu-MOF after introducing silver cluster were specifically discussed, and the study of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF as a heterogeneous catalyst was also briefly explored.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Single-crystal structures
