## 2.2 Characterizations
Ag NCs@Eu-MOF compound was prepared by a solution infiltration method and characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analysis. X-ray photoelectron
spectroscopy (XPS), and synchrotron-radiation based X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS). In this work, Ag NCs@Eu-MOF\(X\) \((X=1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6,\) and 7 days) where \(X\) represents the growth time of the sample. Unless stated, Ag NCs@Eu-MOF refers to Ag NCs@Eu-MOF-7 (the growth time of sample was 7 days).
XRD patterns presented that the experimental Eu-MOF matches well with the simulated data, indicating the phase purity (Fig. 2(f)). In terms of PXRD spectra, the main diffraction peaks of PXRD of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF still corresponded with the simulated one, suggesting obtained Ag NCs@Eu-MOF retains the topology structure of Eu-MOF basically. The introduction of Ag NCs has no effect on the structural integrity of Eu-MOF. There are striking differences between silver ion and europium ion in coordination numbers. Hence, it can be reasonably excluded the possibility that Ag takes the place of Eu to coordinate with oxygen on the carboxyl group.
Theoretically, the structures of ligand, Eu-MOF, and Ag NCs@Eu-MOF were analysed and characterized by FT-IR. According to the FT-IR spectra, the peak at \(1,706\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) assigned to the \(C{=}O\) stretching vibration of the ligand could not be observed in that of Eu-MOF revealing successful coordination of carboxyl groups in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ebdc}\) with europium ions [45]. The characteristic absorption peak of the alkynyl group of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ebdc}\) can be found in Eu-MOF spectrum, located at \(3,300~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , indicating that the alkynyl group is retained as the active group (Fig. S3(a) in the ESM). The results of FT-IR implicate that the asymmetric stretching vibration absorption peaks of the carbonyl groups in MOFs are shifted to lower wavenumbers, which further demonstrates the successful synthesis of MOFs. The similar vibrational characteristics are seen in the FT-IR spectra of EuMOF and Ag NCs@Eu-MOF. The stretching vibrations of the \(C{=}C\) bonds in the aromatic ring, \(C{\in}C\) triple bond, and aromatic \(\mathrm{C-H}\) bonds can be found at 1,500, 2,100, and \(3,100~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , respectively (Fig. S3(b) in the ESM). The most notable difference between Eu-MOF and Ag NCs@Eu-MOF is the disappearance of the \(\mathrm{C-H}\) vibration of the alkynyl group appearing at \(3,300\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in Eu-MOF, indicating Ag could coordinate with the terminal alkynyl of the original Eu-MOF.
To better comprehend the difference of thermal stability between Eu-MOF and Ag NCs@Eu-MOF, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted under nitrogen atmosphere. From the TGA curve of Eu-MOF, there are two distinct weight loss stages. The first stage of weightlessness attributes to the loss of coordinated water molecules and free water in the range of
To better comprehend the difference of thermal stability between Eu-MOF and Ag NCs@Eu-MOF, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted under nitrogen atmosphere. From the TGA curve of Eu-MOF, there are two distinct weight loss stages. The first stage of weightlessness attributes to the loss of coordinated water molecules and free water in the range of \(25{-}135\;\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . The amounts of solvent molecules in the Eu-MOF were determined by TGA, and the theoretical \((7.93\%)\) and experimental values \((7.42\%)\) were in good agreement. The major frameworks Eu-MOF can be maintained basically under the temperature of \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Thermogravimetric results indicate that the thermal stability of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF is improved obviously compared to the initial framework below \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S4 in the ESM). From perspective of decomposition speed, it is important to note that Ag NCs@Eu-MOF decomposes more rapidly than EuMOF above \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) which might results from the metastable structure [46] of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF. Moreover, the remaining residue of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF over \(600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is more than that of EuMOF, which is consistent with the high silver content in Ag NCs@Eu-MOF as reflected in the ICP test results (Table S3 in the ESM).
The distribution and size of Ag NCs were demonstrated by TEM. Figure 2(a) shows a distinct illustration of the uniform distribution of Ag NCs in the parent Eu-MOF. The lattice fringes of the Ag NCs@Eu-MOF are arranged regularly and distinct, and the lattice spacing is \(0.223\;\mathrm{nm}\) , which matches well with the (112) crystal face (Fig. 2(b) and the inset). The average diameter of Ag NCs is \(4.1~\pm~0.1~\mathrm{\nm}\) by Gaussian fitting (Fig. 2(e)). The morphology of the prepared Eu-MOF and Ag NCs@Eu-MOF was observed with SEM. SEM image showed that Ag NCs@Eu-MOF had a rod-like microcrystalline morphology (Fig. 2(c)), which was almost identical to the parent Eu-MOF (Fig. S5 in the ESM). The damage caused by solution impregnation process to the framework was minimal or even negligible, which was confirmed by XRD results (Fig. 2(f)). The composition of the Ag NCs@Eu-MOF has been determined by energy dispersive X-ray (EDX), indicating the presence of the elements Ag, Eu, C, O, and N (Fig. 2(d)). Meanwhile, Ag was uniformly distributed in a single Ag NCs@Eu-MOF microcrystal characterized by elemental mapping of EDX. What's more, no obvious particles can be found on the external surface of the Ag NCs@Eu-MOF, implying that the Ag NCs were confined in Eu-MOF.
XPS was also conducted on Ag NCs@Eu-MOF so as to better illustrate the presence of Ag element in the Ag NCs@Eu-MOF
(Fig. 3(a)). The \(\mathrm{Ag}\ 3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag}\ 3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) binding energy peaks of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF appeared at 374.7 and \(368.7\ \mathrm{\eV}\) [46, 47] as illustrated in Fig. 3(b). To further understand the interaction between Eu-MOF and Ag NCs, the local information of Ag atoms in Ag NCs@Eu-MOF was studied by synchrotron-radiation based XAS. As shown in Figs. 3(c) and 3(d), the X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) and Fourier transformed extended X-ray absorption fine structure (FT-EXAFS) spectra of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF measured at the Ag K-edge were compared with those of the standards Ag and \(\mathrm{PhC}{\equiv}\mathrm{CAg},\) and it can be seen that the Ag atoms in Ag NCs@Eu-MOF appear in a microenvironment similar to those of the standards Ag and \(\mathrm{PhC}{\equiv}\mathrm{CAg}.\) In Fig. 3(c), as can be speculated from the trend of the XANES energy spectra of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF, Ag foil, and \(\mathrm{PhC}{\equiv}\mathrm{CAg},\) the Ag element in the Ag NCs@Eu-MOF has both 0 and \(+1\) valence states. Part of the zero valence silver in the sample may be attributed to the selfreducing property of the alkynyl ligand [48]. The results of highresolution XPS spectrum of \(\mathrm{Ag}\ 3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) are in accord with the result as well (Fig. 3d in the ESM). In Fig. 3(d), the Ag–C \((1.34\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , \(\mathrm{PhC}{\equiv}\mathrm{CAg}\) as the reference) and \(\mathrm{Ag-Ag}\) \((2.16{-}2.73\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , Ag as the reference) scattering paths were found by comparing the Ag NCs@Eu-MOF with the two standard samples, showing that there are coordination bonds of \(\mathrm{Ag-C}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag-Ag}\) in Ag NCs@Eu-MOF.
Subsequently, to study the growth of Ag NCs within the EuMOF, a series of time-dependent ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectra of Ag NCs confined in Eu-MOF were obtained, the position changes of characteristic absorption peaks of Ag NCs@EuMOF were compared (Fig. 3(e)). The absorption peak of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF generates markedly red shift in ultraviolet–visible absorption spectrum with the treatment time prolonged. However, there is no characteristic surface plasmon resonance peak at \(400{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}\) , which indicates the synthesized nanoclusters
rather than nanoparticles [49]. By monitoring the fluorescence changes of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF under different treatment time with fluorescence emission spectra (Fig. 3(f)), the fluorescence intensity of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF decreases dramatically, especially the centre emission of the ligand \((\sim492\ \mathrm{nm})\) , which could be attributed to the interaction between Ag NCs and parent framework (the inset in Fig. 3(f)).
## 2.3 Luminescent thermometer
To further determine the electronic structures, the room temperature solid state fluorescence properties of Eu-MOF have been discussed and investigated. Notably, excited by \(395\,\mathrm{nm}\) , there is a wide blue-emitting peak centred at \(475\,\mathrm{nm}\) which comes from the ligand, as shown in Figs. S7(a) and S7(c) in the ESM. Furthermore, under an excitation wavelength of \(395\,\mathrm{nm}\) , the maximum emission peak of MOF is located at \(611\ \mathrm{nm}\) , corresponding to the characteristic red emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) from \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\) to \({}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2},\) with an absolute photoluminescence quantum yield (PLQY) of \(4.73\%\) . Therefore, the MOF finally displays light orange luminescence under ultraviolet light \((365\ \mathrm{nm})\) , which is visible to the naked eye.
The solid state photoluminescent excitation and emission spectra of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF were also measured, as shown in Fig. S7(b) in the ESM. The emission spectra showed that the ligand centred emission is conspicuously suppressed and the fluorescence intensity is reduced after anchoring Ag NCs. Compared to the emission spectrum of Eu-MOF, the emission peak of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF widens remarkably. The emission intensity is reduced to \(20\%\) of the original one with the Commission Internationale de l'Eclairage,CIE coordinates from (0.2482, 0.2696) to (0.3785, 0.3703) (Fig. S7(f) in the ESM). The fluorescence intensity of ligand decreased and the fluorescence emission peak was red-shifted. Remarkably, despite centre emission of the ligand is significantly decreased, the characteristic peaks of rare earth \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) remain unchanged and appear at 579, 591, 611, 616, and \(699\,\mathrm{nm}\) , corresponding with \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}{-}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{7}\) \((J\,=\,0{-}4)\) transitions, respectively. In addition, the effect of Ag NCs on the luminescence of Ag NCs@Eu-MOF was evaluated by the fluorescence decay time of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) . As can be seen from Figs. S7(d) and S7(e) in the ESM, the luminescence lifetimes of Ag NCs@EuMOF reduced significantly from 412.69 to \(175.60\ \ \upmu\mathrm{s}\) after anchoring Ag NCs, which coincides with photoluminescence (PL) spectral results (Fig. S7(b) in the ESM).
Considering the features of Eu-MOF fluorescence dual emission, the temperature dependent photoluminescence spectra \((25{-}300\,\mathrm{K})\) were measured (Fig. 4(a)). Although the luminescence intensity of both ligand and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) of the Eu-MOF decreased with the increasing temperature, there are obvious differences between the two ones. Thereinto, the characteristic emission intensity of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) \(\mathrm{^{\left(7D_{0}\right.}}\) to \({}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2},\) \(611\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) decreased rapidly with the increase of temperature, and its intensity was quenched by \(78\%\) when the temperature reached \(300\ \mathrm{K},\) which was significantly higher than that of ligand emission \((56\%)\) . Moreover, in the range of \(75{-}275\;\mathrm{~K},\) the decline rate of emission intensity of Eu-MOF reached \(0.33\%\) , which was significantly higher than that of ligand emission in the same range \((0.14\%)\) . Since the emission intensity of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions and ligands decreases at different rates with increasing temperature, the emission intensity ratio of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) and ligand can be used as a ratio parameter to realize the self-calibration detection of temperature (Fig. 4(b)). The temperature-dependent photoluminescence spectra of Eu-MOF indicated that the ratio of the characteristic emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) \(\left(E_{\mathrm{Eu}}\right)\) to the central emission of the ligand \(\left(I_{\mathrm{L}}\right)\) showed a good linear relationship within the temperature range from \(75{-}275\ \mathrm{K}.\) The fitting standard curve of \(\mathrm{Eu}\) and \(I_{\mathrm{L}}\) with temperature was calibrated, of which the
correlation coefficient of the standard curve was 0.9875, and the fitting equation was as follows (Fig. 4(c))
\[
\Delta=10.12347-0.01561T
\]
where \(\Delta\ =\ I_{\mathrm{Eu}}/I_{\mathrm{L}},\) \(I_{\mathrm{Eu}}\) and \(I_{\mathrm{L}}\) are emission intensities of the characteristic emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{\mathrm{3+}}\) \((611~\mathrm{\nm})\) and ligand centred emission \((492\,\mathrm{nm})\) in Eu-MOF, respectively.
Relative thermal sensitivity \((S_{\mathrm{R}})\) of Eu-MOF between 75–275 K can be calculated in the following equation
\[
S_{\mathrm{R}}=\frac{1}{\Delta(T)}\left|\frac{\partial\Delta(T)}{\partial T}\right|\times100\left(\%\cdot\mathrm{K}^{-1}\right)
\]
\[
\begin{array}{c}{\displaystyle=\frac{b}{\left(I_{\mathrm{Eu}}/I_{\mathrm{L}}\times\left(T=275\mathrm{K}\right)-I_{\mathrm{Eu}}/I_{\mathrm{L}}\times\left(T=75\mathrm{K}\right)\right)}\times100\left(\%\cdot\mathrm{K}^{-1}\right)}\\{\displaystyle=1.539\left(\%\cdot\mathrm{K}^{-1}\right)}\end{array}
\]
The temperature-dependent luminescence colors of Eu-MOF changes slowly, which the corresponding CIE coordinates change from (0.4546, 0.3701) at \(25~\mathrm{K}\) to (0.3906, 0.3672) at \(300~\mathrm{K}\) (Fig. 4(d)). Consequently, the good fluorescence detection performance of the Eu-MOF in the low temperature region of \(75{-}275\;\mathrm{~K~}\) provides the possibility for its application in self-calibration luminescent thermometer.
## 2.4 \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) cycloaddition
made to expand the range of substrates in the cycloaddition reaction with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (Table S6 in the ESM). When several typical epoxides reacted with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) under the same conditions, the corresponding cyclic carbonates were also obtained in good to excellent yields. The integrity of the main framework of catalyst Ag NCs@Eu-MOF-1 was maintained after the catalytic reaction, which was proved by PXRD results (Fig. S8 in the ESM). TEM images of the samples before and after the catalytic reaction also proved that the highly dispersed Ag NCs in the MOF were also well maintained (Fig. S9 in the ESM). ICP analysis of the filtered reaction solution showed no leakage of Ag element (below the detection limit) and a very low leakage of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) from the parent framework \((1.7\ \mathrm{\ppm})\) . Overall, Ag NCs@Eu-MOF shows the potential application in cycloaddition of epoxides with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) and provides a reference for the synthesis of new efficient metal complex catalytic system as well.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, for the first time, we successfully prepared a photoluminescent Eu-MOF by the method of hydrothermal synthesis, and a host–guest composite Ag NCs@ Eu-MOF in a simple and mild solution immersion way. The as-prepared EuMOF exhibits excellent temperature dependent photoluminescence properties and great development potential in temperature sensor. In addition, with carbon dioxide involved, the obtained Ag NCs@Eu-MOF as catalysts can be efficiently converted epoxides into to cyclic carbonates, showing excellent catalytic capability. The host–guest interaction between the luminescent MOF and the guest Ag NCs is very fascinating and interesting, which will provide experimental reference and theoretical model of metal clusters@MOFs.
